{
    // Mutation testing for the Doctrine listener, kept apart from infection.json5.
    //
    // Two things make it its own run rather than another directory in that one. The
    // tests are slow — each builds an EntityManager and a schema — so a mutant here
    // needs a timeout that would let a genuinely looping mutant in the redactor run for
    // a minute before anyone noticed. And the run takes minutes rather than a minute,
    // which is fine for a job of its own and not fine for the one somebody waits on.
    //
    // Nothing from the other file's ignore list is repeated here, and nothing needs to
    // be: those entries name classes this run does not mutate.
    $schema: "tools/infection/vendor/infection/infection/resources/schema.json",
    source: {
        directories: [
            "src/Doctrine",
        ],
    },
    logs: {
        text: "var/infection/doctrine-escaped.log",
        summary: "var/infection/doctrine-summary.log",
        // What tools/infection/gate.php checks a part against the plan with.
        json: "var/infection/doctrine.json",
    },
    mutators: {
        "@default": true,

        // No ignores, and that is a finding rather than an omission. Every one that stood here
        // named a method or a line of the listener before 1.3 rewrote it -- twenty-four, each
        // pointing at a method that is gone or a line outside its method -- so none of them
        // ignored anything, and the scores of 1.3 were measured with all of them generated.
        // One of them was also wrong by then: the finally in beginFlush() was called the same
        // as the line after it because the late write's failures were swallowed, and under
        // on_failure: throw they are not -- ALateFlushBehindAFailedOneTest now says what
        // leaving it out cost.
        //
        // Nothing is ignored here. A mutation is left out of the count only by a record in
        // tools/infection/exclusions.json, argued from PHP or from Doctrine and DBAL, which
        // gate.php holds to the plan made without it. The one ignore that stood here --
        // ReadableSql::of()'s early return, the reading after it giving the same text -- was
        // an argument from this bundle's own code, and such a mutant stays in the count.
    },
    // Its own floor, and well below the other run's, because this listener is the code
    // the bundle calls its price of complexity: one class answering for every shape a
    // Doctrine flush can take.
    //
    // 97 against 99.15 measured, which is the same arrangement the other file argues
    // for: a floor set at the best observed number turns ordinary drift into a job that
    // fails for no reason, and a check nobody trusts is worse than none.
    //
    // It moves up a group of escaped mutants at a time, the way that one did. 72 escaped
    // when this was first measured honestly; the flush bookkeeping, the always-recorded
    // context, the walk over an element's owners, what a change inside an element is
    // built from, the wording of every refused declaration, what an abandoned flush
    // counted, which keys a corrected element owns, what an identifier that is not a
    // scalar becomes and what a collection Doctrine keys is recorded as have been
    // worked through since, and 6 are left. Four arrived with the three fixes of 1.2.4
    // and three of them were worked through in the same breath; the fourth is the `??`
    // on the context an owner kept when Doctrine gave it no event, which no arrangement
    // of fixtures reaches — an owner that emptied a collection *and* was updated has a
    // record already, so it never takes that road — and which therefore stays escaping
    // rather than being claimed equivalent. Sixteen of the escaped mutants turned out
    // to be equivalent by construction and are listed above with the reason. Each
    // commit that takes a group names what remains.
    //
    // The line numbers above go stale when a method moves, the mutant is generated
    // again and this job goes red. That is the arrangement working, not breaking: it
    // happened on the commit that taught the listener about a flush whose only history
    // came from inside a collection, and every claim had to be pointed at its line
    // again — which is the moment to notice if one of them stopped being true.
    // Lowered from 97 to 96 for 1.3.0, against 96.05 measured, and the reason is written
    // here rather than worked around in the code.
    //
    // Reading the membership of an emptied collection from the rows instead of from
    // Doctrine's snapshot fixed five defects and took a reader away: the question "are the
    // rows gone" was asked twice, and one of the two askers is gone. What is left of it is
    // only reached by a flush that emptied a collection and ran no statement of its own,
    // which an inverse collection with orphanRemoval never is -- so one arm of
    // CollectionRowsQuery is no longer reached from the listener at all, and the mutants in
    // the lines that feed it cannot be killed from here. That is seven of the forty. They
    // are not claimed equivalent, because they are not: the arm is live code with tests of
    // its own in CollectionRowsQueryTest, and only this caller stopped using it.
    //
    // Raising the floor back is a question of whether that arm has a caller, not of
    // writing more tests for this class, and inventing a caller to keep a number is the
    // thing this file exists to argue against. Measured with a probe: a test written to
    // reach that arm passed without reaching it, which is how it was noticed.
    // 94, and this job is RED against it on purpose: 92.7 measured (86 of 1177).
    //
    // It was lowered 96 -> 94 once with a reason, and that reason was wrong. It said seven
    // of the escaping mutants were structural because the arm of CollectionRowsQuery that
    // reads the elements' table was reached by nothing in the listener -- measured with a
    // probe. The probe had walked one road, a fresh emptying. The arm was taken out on
    // that basis, and the search found the road it had missed within one run of three
    // thousand sequences: a stale deletion, handed after a swallowed postFlush to a flush
    // with nothing else to do, asks exactly that question. The arm is back, the sequence
    // that found it is a regression, and those seven mutants are mostly killed by it.
    //
    // What is red now is new code with one guard where it needs three: the log that lets
    // a nested flush which dies put back what it swept out of the flushes still running.
    // It rolls back three maps -- arrivals, changes, emptyings -- and a test reaches only
    // the changes. Tests written for the other two passed without reaching the log, and
    // were taken out rather than kept as guards that guard nothing. Whether those two can
    // be reached at all is an open question, and it is not going to be answered by saying
    // "unreachable" an hour after that word turned out wrong about the arm above.
    //
    // So the floor is not lowered a third time to make the job pass. Both reviewers of this
    // round said as much, and they were right: two lowerings in three days is the tool not
    // seeing what kills the mutants, not the code getting harder. A release waits for this
    // job, which is what release.yml's gate is for.
    //
    // What the score still does not do is stand in for the search: three thousand
    // sequences run green against the rows, and the list of known failures is empty.
    minCoveredMsi: 94,
    // High enough that nothing is skipped — see the other configuration's for why that is
    // what decides it, and tools/infection/gate.php for the refusal that holds it there.
    // The listener's lines are covered by the whole Doctrine suite, and at 60 seconds 1,176
    // of 3,842 mutants were skipped uncounted while 337 were called timeouts, each of which
    // counts as a kill; at 120 none were skipped and 31 timed out. Too low a timeout lifts
    // the score both ways, by what it leaves out and by what it calls killed.
    timeout: 120,
    // As in the other configuration: the memory bar is a measurement, and the group list
    // replaces phpunit.xml.dist's. The model of the rows stays — it is what kills the
    // mutants no single test describes.
    testFrameworkExtraArgs: "--exclude-group=integration --exclude-group=benchmark --exclude-group=budget",
}
